= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Over $\overline{\mathbb F}_3$, take the projective cubic
$$
\boxed{C=V_+(Y^2Z-X^3+XZ^2)\subseteq\mathbb P^2.}
$$
In characteristic three its partial <derivatives> are $F_X=Z^2$, $F_Y=2YZ$ and $F_Z=Y^2+2XZ$. If they vanished simultaneously, $Z=0$ and then $Y=0$. The equation of $C$ would force $X=0$, impossible for a projective point. Thus the <Jacobian criterion> proves smoothness everywhere, including infinity.

It is an irreducible cubic: on the affine chart $Z=1$, the polynomial is $y^2-(x^3-x)$, and $x^3-x$ is not a square in $\overline{\mathbb F}_3(x)$ because its pole at infinity has odd order three. A quadratic in $y$ would factor over that field only if its right side were a square. <Gauss lemma for polynomials> then gives affine irreducibility, and homogenization introduces no factor at infinity because $Z$ does not divide $F$. This supplies the <smooth plane cubic in characteristic three>. In particular, choosing a Fermat cubic here would fail, because its cubic partial <derivatives> vanish in characteristic three.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Take the <projective hypersurface>
$$
\boxed{S=V_+(X_0X_1-X_2^2)\subseteq\mathbb P^3_{\mathbb C}.}
$$
As a polynomial in $X_0$ over $\mathbb C[X_1,X_2,X_3]$, it is primitive because $X_1$ and $X_2^2$ are coprime. It is linear and irreducible over the fraction field, so <Gauss lemma for polynomials> proves irreducibility. Thus it is an irreducible surface.

Its gradient is $(X_1,X_0,-2X_2,0)$. The simultaneous zero set on $S$ is exactly
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Sing}S=\{[0:0:0:1]\}.}
$$
The <Jacobian criterion> proves every other point is smooth. At that vertex the affine equation in the chart $X_3=1$ has no linear term, so the tangent space has dimension three whereas the surface has dimension two; the vertex is genuinely singular. This is a <projective quadric cone with one singular vertex>.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take the <plane quartic with exactly two nodes>
$$
\boxed{C=V_+\bigl((X^2-Z^2)^2+XZY^2+Y^4\bigr)\subseteq\mathbb P^2_{\mathbb C}.}
$$
Put $d=X^2-Z^2$. Its partial <derivatives> are
$$
F_X=4Xd+ZY^2,\qquad F_Z=-4Zd+XY^2,\qquad F_Y=2XYZ+4Y^3.
$$
On $Y=0$, the equation gives $X^2=Z^2$, producing exactly $P_+=[1:0:1]$ and $P_-=[-1:0:1]$, and their gradients vanish.

There are no singular points with $Y\ne0$. Normalize $Y=1$. The derivative equations give $X=4dZ$ and $(16d^2+1)Z=0$. If $Z=0$, then $X=0$ and $F_Y=4\ne0$, so $Z\ne0$ and $d^2=-1/16$. The remaining equation gives $XZ=-2$, hence $Z^2=-1/(2d)$. But $d=X^2-Z^2=-2Z^2=1/d$, forcing $d^2=1$, a contradiction. Thus **the singular set is exactly $\{P_+,P_-\}$**.

In the chart $Z=1$ near $P_\varepsilon$, put $X=\varepsilon+u$, $Y=v$ with $\varepsilon=\pm1$. The lowest-degree part is $4u^2+\varepsilon v^2$, which splits into two distinct complex linear factors. Each singularity is therefore an <ordinary double point>, with two smooth transverse branches.

It remains to prove irreducibility. A repeated polynomial factor would make every point on that component singular, contradicting the finite singular set, so $F$ is reduced. If it were reducible, partition its irreducible factors into two coprime factors of degrees $r$ and $4-r$, $1\le r\le3$. Every intersection of the two components would be singular on $C$, hence one of the two <ordinary double points>. At an <ordinary double point> shared by them, the two branches are transverse, so its intersection multiplicity is one. Their total intersection multiplicity would therefore be at most two. The <Bézout theorem> instead makes it $r(4-r)\ge3$. This contradiction proves **the displayed quartic is irreducible and has exactly two singular points**.

= 4
{parent=Paper 21}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Being constant on the two-element orbits is equivalent to $f(-x,-y)=f(x,y)$. In characteristic zero, comparing <monomial> coefficients shows that precisely the terms of even total degree survive. Thus a <vector space> <basis> is
$$
\boxed{\{x^{2a}y^{2b},\ x^{2a+1}y^{2b+1}:a,b\ge0\}.}
$$
Set $u=x^2$, $v=xy$, $w=y^2$. The two basis types are $u^aw^b$ and $vu^aw^b$, so they prove $R=k[u,v,w]$. Hence $R$ is a <finitely generated algebra>. It is an <integral domain> as a subring of the <integral domain> $k[x,y]$.

The generators satisfy $uw-v^2=0$. To prove this is the whole relation ideal, reduce any polynomial in $U,V,W$ modulo the monic relation $V^2-UW$ to
$$
A(U,W)+VB(U,W).
$$
Its image in $k[x,y]$ is $A(x^2,y^2)+xyB(x^2,y^2)$. The first summand has both exponents even and the second both odd; their distinct <monomials> are linearly independent. Thus the image vanishes only when both $A,B$ are zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{R\cong k[U,V,W]/(UW-V^2),\qquad Y=V(UW-V^2)\subseteq\mathbb A^3.}
$$
This gives the requested closed embedding and the <quadratic cone invariant ring> presentation.

The associated <morphism of algebraic varieties> is explicitly
$$
f:\mathbb A^2\longrightarrow Y,\qquad (x,y)\longmapsto(x^2,xy,y^2).
$$
It is surjective. For $(u,v,w)\in Y$, if $u\ne0$, choose $x$ with $x^2=u$ using algebraic closedness and put $y=v/x$. Then $y^2=v^2/u=w$. If $u=0$, the relation forces $v=0$; take $x=0$ and choose $y^2=w$. This covers every point of $Y$. Except over the origin, the two preimages differ by the simultaneous sign change, consistent with the orbit description.

Finally the gradient of $UW-V^2$ is $(W,-2V,U)$, so the <Jacobian criterion> makes every nonzero point smooth. At the origin, the maximal ideal modulo its square has the three independent classes of $U,V,W$, since the defining relation is quadratic. The tangent space there has dimension three, while $Y$ has dimension two. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Sing}Y=\{(0,0,0)\}.}
$$

= 5
{parent=Paper 21}
{scope}

= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Use the standard convention that a <projective variety> is nonempty and irreducible, over an <algebraically closed field>. Let $f:X\to\mathbb A^1$ be a <regular function>, and compose with the inclusion into $\mathbb P^1$. Its graph is closed in $X\times\mathbb P^1$: on an affine open of $X$, with homogeneous target coordinates $[T_0:T_1]$, it is given by $T_1-f(x)T_0=0$. This equation has no solution at $T_0=0$, and the local closedness statements glue.

Embed $X$ as a closed subvariety of $\mathbb P^m$. The graph is then closed in $\mathbb P^m\times\mathbb P^1$. Part 1, after swapping factors, makes its projected image $f(X)$ closed in $\mathbb P^1$. Since it avoids infinity, it is a proper closed subset of the projective line and hence finite. A continuous image of an irreducible space is irreducible, and a nonempty finite closed subset over an algebraically closed field is irreducible only if it is a singleton. Hence
$$
\boxed{f\equiv\lambda\text{ for some }\lambda\in k,\qquad H^0(X,\mathcal O_X)=k.}
$$
This proves <global regular functions on an irreducible projective variety> are constant, using the closed-projection result rather than assuming it. If one allows disconnected projective algebraic sets instead of irreducible varieties, different components can carry different constants; irreducibility is the relevant convention here.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Let $\phi:C\setminus S\to\mathbb P^n$ be the given algebraic <morphism of algebraic varieties>. Its value at the generic point supplies homogeneous coordinates $[h_0:\cdots:h_n]$ with $h_i\in k(C)$, not all zero. Thus it defines a <rational map of projective varieties> on $C$.

Fix a missing point $p\in S$. The <local ring of a smooth algebraic curve> at $p$ is a <discrete valuation ring> $A=\mathcal O_{C,p}$, with <uniformizer> $t$ and <valuation> $v_p$. Put
$$
m=\min_{h_i\ne0}v_p(h_i),\qquad a_i=t^{-m}h_i.
$$
Zero coordinates remain zero. Every $a_i$ is in $A$, and some $a_j$ is a unit because its valuation is zero. Choose a neighborhood $U$ of $p$ on which all $a_i$ are regular and $a_j$ remains nonvanishing; this is possible because membership in the local ring means regularity on a neighborhood. The affine target-chart coordinates
$$
\frac{a_i}{a_j}\qquad(i\ne j)
$$
are regular on $U$, so they define a <morphism of algebraic varieties> $U\to\mathbb P^n$. At $p$ its homogeneous value is $[a_0(p):\cdots:a_n(p)]$, well-defined because $a_j(p)\ne0$.

Multiplication of all homogeneous coordinates by the same nonzero <rational function> does not change their projective point. Thus this local morphism agrees with the original one on the common dense open where their coordinates were chosen. They agree throughout the overlap: the target's diagonal is closed, being defined in homogeneous coordinate pairs by $T_iU_j-T_jU_i=0$ for all $i,j$. Thus the equalizer is closed and contains that dense open. Apply this construction at each point of the finite set $S$ and glue with the original morphism. The same closed-diagonal argument proves uniqueness. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\phi\text{ extends uniquely to a morphism }C\to\mathbb P^n.}
$$
This gives the <extension of a rational map from a smooth projective curve> by explicit local coordinates. The mechanism is the one-dimensional smooth local ring: it permits removal of the smallest common pole order while leaving at least one unit coordinate.
